# BFGS

BFGS is a local optimizer for smooth functions when you can compute a gradient.
It builds a dense approximation to the inverse Hessian from changes in the
gradient, using that model to scale and combine search directions. You do not
need to supply a Hessian, but storing the model requires memory proportional to
the square of the number of parameters.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/bfgs/struct.Bfgs.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                              |
  | ----------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
  | Objective   | A smooth scalar function                                 |
  | Evaluations | `CostFunction` and `Gradient`                            |
  | Search      | Local, from one starting point                           |
  | Constraints | Unconstrained; use [L-BFGS-B](../l-bfgs-b/) for box bounds |
  | Parameters  | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64`             |

## When to use it

Consider BFGS when gradients are available and a dense inverse-Hessian
approximation fits comfortably in memory. It can use curvature information to
make more progress per iteration than steepest descent. The method and its
line-search requirements are described in [Nocedal and Wright
(2006)](#ref-nocedal-wright-2006), Chapter 6.

For a larger problem, [L-BFGS](../l-bfgs/) retains a short history of curvature
pairs instead of a dense matrix. If you can supply useful Hessian-vector
products, compare [trust-region Newton](../../#trust-region). For objectives
expressed through residuals and a Jacobian, consider the [nonlinear least-squares
methods](../../#nonlinear-least-squares).

BFGS follows local descent directions, so its starting point matters. Noisy or
nonsmooth gradients can also make its curvature model unreliable. Compare
[derivative-free methods](../../#derivative-free) when a reliable gradient is
unavailable.

## How it works

Each iteration multiplies the negative gradient by the current inverse-Hessian
approximation to obtain a search direction. A line search then chooses how far
to move along it. Basin uses a
[strong Wolfe line search](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/line_search/wolfe/struct.Wolfe.html)
by default, seeking sufficient decrease and a curvature condition. One solver
iteration can therefore require several objective and gradient evaluations.

After an accepted step, BFGS compares the change in parameters with the change
in gradient. A rank-two update adjusts the model to match this new curvature
information while preserving symmetry. With positive curvature and a positive
definite initial model, the update preserves positive definiteness, making the
next direction a descent direction in exact arithmetic.

Basin starts with the identity matrix and rescales it immediately before the
first accepted curvature update. Updates that fail the curvature safeguard are
skipped, leaving the current model in place.

## Example

This quadratic has its minimum at `[1.0, -2.0]`. The example supplies an
analytic gradient, sets a gradient tolerance on the solver, and limits
iterations through the executor.

<!-- compile: solver-bfgs -->
```rust
use basin::{Bfgs, CostFunction, Executor, Gradient, Termination};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Quadratic;

impl CostFunction for Quadratic {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok((x[0] - 1.0).powi(2) + 4.0 * (x[1] + 2.0).powi(2))
    }
}

impl Gradient for Quadratic {
    type Gradient = Vec<f64>;

    fn gradient(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<Self::Gradient, Self::Error> {
        Ok(vec![2.0 * (x[0] - 1.0), 8.0 * (x[1] + 2.0)])
    }
}

fn main() {
    let solver = Bfgs::new().with_absolute_gradient_tolerance(1e-8);

    let result = Executor::from_start(Quadratic, solver, vec![-3.0, 5.0])
        .max_iter(200)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!(matches!(
        result.report.termination,
        Termination::Converged(_)
    ));
    assert!((result.param()[0] - 1.0).abs() < 1e-7);
    assert!((result.param()[1] + 2.0).abs() < 1e-7);
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Allow for dense storage

The inverse-Hessian model needs `O(n²)` storage and work per update for `n`
parameters. Check this cost before choosing BFGS for a large problem. L-BFGS
lets you limit the stored curvature history instead.

### Scale the parameters and the initial model

Rescaling parameters can improve the model's starting scale and make gradient
tolerances easier to interpret. If you already have a useful approximation,
`with_initial_inverse_hessian(matrix)` supplies a fixed seed, while
`with_initial_inverse_hessian_initializer(initializer)` constructs one from
the initial point and gradient. Custom seeds skip the default first-update
rescaling so their scaling survives.

### Configure a stopping test

BFGS has no default optimality check. Use
`with_absolute_gradient_tolerance` or `with_relative_gradient_tolerance` to
test the Euclidean gradient norm. Choose the threshold for your parameter units,
precision, and derivative accuracy; `1e-8` is a choice for the example above.
Optional step and cost-change checks can also stop a run, but a small observed
change alone does not establish stationarity.

`with_relative_curvature_tolerance` controls whether the model accepts an
update. It is an algorithm safeguard, not a convergence tolerance. Keep
iteration and evaluation budgets on the executor, then inspect the termination
report to distinguish convergence from a budget limit or a line-search failure.

## Implementation

BFGS publishes the current point, objective, and gradient through
`FirstOrderState`; the solver owns the inverse-Hessian model. Its matrix type
is inferred from the parameter backend through `DenseBackend`. Fresh solves
rebuild the model and reset the line search; exact solver-and-state checkpoints
retain both. Use `Executor::run_with_solver` to retain the final solver and
inspect `inverse_hessian()`.

You can supply another strategy through `Bfgs::with_line_search(search)`.
Choose a strategy that provides the curvature conditions the update needs.

Supported parameter types are `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. The default
model uses matrix-vector products and rank-one updates without a factorization
or a BLAS/LAPACK provider. Fixed initial matrices additionally require cloning
and entry access; closure initializers require entry access. Basin checks these
seeds for matching dimensions, finiteness, exact symmetry, and positive
definiteness, using a pure-Rust Cholesky check. Invalid seeds panic at
initialization. Named initializers enforce their own seed contracts. See the
API's [Backends
section](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/bfgs/struct.Bfgs.html#backends)
for the exact capability requirements.

